Subject: [PATCH] msync: fall through to server for NtPulseEvent on non-msync handles

msync_pulse_event returned get_object()'s raw error. When the handle names a
server-only object (e.g. a keyed event) or otherwise lacks the SYNCHRONIZE
access that the get_msync_idx server round-trip demands, that error is
STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED, which was surfaced to the caller directly instead of
letting the wineserver apply the correct per-op semantics.

Return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED on any unresolved handle so NtPulseEvent falls
through to the server (which yields STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH for a keyed
event), and reject non-event msync objects up front like msync_set_event does.

Fixes ntdll:sync test:338 (NtPulseEvent on a keyed-event handle must return
STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH, not STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED).

diff --git a/dlls/ntdll/unix/msync.c b/dlls/ntdll/unix/msync.c
index 20ed3ae2..555927cb 100644
--- a/dlls/ntdll/unix/msync.c
+++ b/dlls/ntdll/unix/msync.c
@@ -1228,13 +1228,22 @@ NTSTATUS msync_pulse_event( HANDLE handle, LONG *prev )
     struct event *event;
     struct msync *obj;
     LONG current;
-    NTSTATUS ret;
 
     TRACE("%p.\n", handle);
 
-    if ((ret = get_object( handle, &obj ))) return ret;
+    /* If the handle can't be resolved as an msync object (e.g. it names a
+     * server-only object such as a keyed event, or lacks the SYNCHRONIZE access
+     * that get_msync_idx demands), fall through to the server so it can apply the
+     * correct per-op semantics -- NtPulseEvent on a non-event must yield
+     * STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH, not the raw STATUS_ACCESS_DENIED msync would
+     * otherwise surface (ntdll sync test:338). */
+    if (get_object( handle, &obj )) return STATUS_NOT_IMPLEMENTED;
     event = obj->shm;
 
+    /* Reject non-event msync objects like msync_set_event does. */
+    if (obj->type != MSYNC_MANUAL_EVENT && obj->type != MSYNC_AUTO_EVENT)
+        return STATUS_OBJECT_TYPE_MISMATCH;
+
     /* This isn't really correct; an application could miss the write.
      * Unfortunately we can't really do much better. Fortunately this is rarely
      * used (and publicly deprecated). */
